{"page_content": "Propellants, non- motive (blowing agents) Reactive cleaning/removal agent Reducing agent Refrigerants Resins (prepolymers) Semiconductor and photovoltaic agent Sizing agent Softener Substance that is used for expelling products from pressurized containers (aerosol products); used to dissolve or suspend other substances and either to expel those substances from a container in the form of an aerosol or to impart a cellular structure to plastics, rubber, or thermo set resins; provides the force necessary to expel the contents of aerosol containers; liquefied or compressed gas within which substances are dissolved or suspended and expelled from a container upon discharge of the internal pressure through expansion of the gas. The formulated product in the pressurized container may be solution, emulsion, or suspension. Substance that reacts with and removes surface contaminants and is generally consumed, e.g., oxides, sulfides. Substance that during reactions with oxidizing agents lose electrons; commonly contributes hydrogen to other substances; used to remove oxygen, hydrogenate or, in general, acts as electron donor in chemical reactions. Substances used within machines such as air conditioning units, refrigerators, and walk in freezers to cool indoor air and reduce temperatures. Usually high molecular weight polymers that lower viscosity. Thermoplastic resins soften when exposed to heat and return to original form at room temperature, and thermosetting resins solidify irreversibly when heated due to cross-linking. Substance that has resistivity between that of insulators and metals; usually changeable by light, heat or electrical or magnetic field; generates electromotive force upon the incidence of radiant energy. Substance applied to substrates such as fabric, yarn, paper products, or plaster to increase abrasive resistance, stiffness, strength, smoothness, or reduce absorption. Substance used for softening materials to improve feel, to facilitate"